[Id "scandinavian"]
[Name "Scandinavian Defense"]
[Side "black"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Description "Hit e4 on move one with 1…d5, then a solid …c6 and …Bf5 set-up."]
[Tags "beginner-friendly, solid"]

1. e4 d5 {…d5 challenges e4 at once, so White has no time to build a big centre.} 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3
  (3. d4 {@variation Quiet 3.d4 / 3.Nf3} 3... Nf6 {…Nf6 develops; the queen is safe on d5 for now.} 4. Nf3 Bf5 {…Bf5 gets the bishop out before …e6.} 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O c6 7. c4 Qd8 {…Qd8 retreats home when c4 hits the queen; the position is solid.} 8. Nc3 Be7 {…Be7 develops and gets ready to castle.} (8... Nbd7 {@alt})
{@line id=scandinavian-3d4 level=1 tier=main name="3.d4" summary="Same set-up without Nc3: …Nf6, …Bf5, …e6, …c6, and retreat the queen to d8 when kicked."}
)
  (3. Nf3 {@variation Quiet 3.d4 / 3.Nf3} 3... Nf6 {…Nf6 develops; after d4 this is the same as the 3.d4 line.} 4. d4 Bf5 {…Bf5: the position is now identical to 3.d4 Nf6 4.Nf3 Bf5.} 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O c6 7. c4 Qd8 8. Nc3 Be7 (8... Nbd7 {@alt})
{@line id=scandinavian-3nf3 level=1 tier=side name="3.Nf3" summary="3.Nf3 and 3.d4 lead to the same position: one plan for both."}
)
3... Qa5 {…Qa5 steps out of the knight's attack and pins the c3 knight against the king.} {@variation Main Line} 4. d4
  (4. Nf3 Nf6 {…Nf6: the usual move, whatever White's order.} 5. d4 c6 {…c6: the position is now the same as the main line.} 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6
{@line id=scandinavian-4nf3 level=2 tier=main name="4.Nf3 move order" summary="4.Nf3 transposes: the set-up …Nf6, …c6, …Bf5, …e6 doesn't change."}
)
4... Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 {…c6 gives the queen a retreat and stops Nb5 or Bb5 tricks.} 6. Bc4 Bf5 {…Bf5 develops the bishop outside the pawn chain before …e6.} 7. Bd2
  (7. Ne5 e6 {…e6 makes room for the bishop and opens the f8 bishop.} 8. g4 Bg6 {…Bg6 keeps the bishop; White attacks it with the h-pawn next.} 9. h4 Nbd7 {…Nbd7 challenges the strong e5 knight so that h5 can be met calmly.}
{@line id=scandinavian-ne5 level=2 tier=side name="7.Ne5" summary="Against Ne5 and the g4/h4 pawn storm, challenge the e5 knight with …Nbd7."}
)
7... e6
{@line id=scandinavian-qa5-main level=1 tier=main name="3.Nc3 Qa5" summary="Queen to a5, then …Nf6, …c6, …Bf5 and …e6: a Caro-Kann set-up with the queen out."}
8. Qe2
  (8. Nd5 {Nd5 discovers an attack from the d2 bishop on the a5 queen.} 8... Qd8 {…Qd8 is the safe retreat; taking with …cxd5 or …exd5 loses to Bxa5.} 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 {…Qxf6 recaptures with an active queen.}
{@line id=scandinavian-nd5-trap level=2 tier=side name="8.Nd5 trick" summary="When Nd5 uncovers Bd2 on your queen, retreat with …Qd8; never capture the knight."}
)
8... Bb4 {…Bb4 pins the c3 knight again and develops.} 9. O-O-O Nbd7 {…Nbd7 develops and covers e5.} 10. a3 Bxc3 11. Bxc3 Qc7 {…Qc7 gets the queen out of the bishop's line and eyes the kingside.}
{@line id=scandinavian-qa5-deep level=2 tier=main name="8.Qe2" summary="Pin with …Bb4, develop with …Nbd7, and trade on c3 when White plays a3."} *
